# 1. Executive Summary

Healthily Me aims to address declining user engagement and improve free-to-paid subscription conversion through a focused Minimum Viable Product (MVP).

Initial investigation indicates that the largest conversion drop-off occurs before users initiate a paid subscription. Meaningful engagement and retention are positively associated with paid conversion, but the underlying causes of non-conversion and the extent to which individual product features influence conversion remain unvalidated.

The project will define and develop a focused set of habit-tracking and engagement capabilities intended to support consistent user activity and help users monitor their progress. The MVP will be evaluated against established baselines to determine whether the proposed changes contribute to improved conversion while remaining within approved budget, scope and delivery constraints.

The primary business objective is to improve free-to-paid subscription conversion. Engagement and retention are supporting outcomes, and the project will evaluate their relationship with conversion rather than assume causation.

The findings will inform whether the solution should be iterated, expanded or discontinued.

# 2. Project Description

## 2.1 Project Background

Healthily Me is a habit-tracking application concept intended to help users record health-related activities, monitor progress and develop consistent routines.

The project focuses on the challenge of sustaining user engagement beyond initial onboarding and evaluating whether improvements in meaningful engagement can support commercial outcomes.

Rather than undertaking a complete product redesign or implementing every proposed feature, the project will prioritize a small, coherent MVP based on business value, available evidence, feasibility, dependencies and risk.

## 2.2 Problem Statement

Users experience declining engagement during the initial weeks after onboarding, and the largest observed subscription conversion drop-off occurs before users initiate a paid subscription.

Reported barriers to continued engagement include repetitive tracking, forgetting to record activities, reduced motivation and difficulty resuming routines after missed days.

Although meaningful engagement is positively associated with retention and paid conversion, the reasons for non-conversion and the impact of potential interventions remain insufficiently validated.

The business therefore needs to investigate and test a focused solution that supports meaningful engagement and evaluates its contribution to paid conversion.

## 2.3 Business Objectives

| ID | Business Objective | Measurement |
|---|---|---|
| BO-01 | Improve free-to-paid subscription conversion. | Conversion rate compared with an established baseline. |
| BO-02 | Support meaningful user engagement. | Agreed habit-activity and engagement metrics. |
| BO-03 | Support user retention beyond the early engagement drop-off period. | Agreed retention measures, including Day-30 retention where appropriate. |
| BO-04 | Validate the commercial value of the proposed intervention before further investment. | Experiment results, implementation costs and financial evaluation. |

**Priority hierarchy:** Paid conversion is the primary objective; engagement and retention are supporting outcomes.

Numerical targets, measurement windows and metric definitions must be validated with Product/Growth, Analytics and Finance before experimentation.

## 2.4 Business Drivers

### Driver 1: Commercial Value

Improve the conversion of existing free users to paid subscribers and evaluate the commercial value generated by the intervention.

### Driver 2: Meaningful Engagement and Retention

Investigate ways to support consistent health-related activity and reduce disengagement during the early user journey.

### Driver 3: Evidence-Based Investment

Validate the effectiveness, feasibility and financial viability of the MVP before committing to a broader release or substantial redesign.

# 3. Project Scope

## 3.1 In Scope — Proposed MVP

The following capabilities are proposed for the initial MVP, subject to prioritization, feasibility assessment and formal approval.

| ID | Capability | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| S-01 | Habit tracking and logging | Allow users to record selected health-related activities and view their progress. |
| S-02 | Streak tracking | Help users monitor consecutive days of habit completion. |
| S-03 | Milestone tracking | Recognize progress toward predefined habit-related milestones. |
| S-04 | Reminders | Support users in maintaining their intended routines. |
| S-05 | Non-monetary XP rewards | Provide recognition for eligible activities and progress. |
| S-06 | Streak pause and grace-period mechanisms | Support continuity and recovery when users temporarily pause or miss activities. |

The final MVP must be the smallest coherent feature set that can test the business hypothesis. Features should be prioritized by evidence, expected value, implementation effort, dependencies and risk.

## 3.2 Out of Scope — Proposed Exclusions

The following capabilities are excluded from the proposed initial MVP unless formally approved through scope review.

| ID | Capability | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| OS-01 | Community groups, friend leaderboards and shared challenges | Higher implementation effort and an unvalidated contribution to the primary business objective. |
| OS-02 | Complete UI or mascot redesign | A major redesign is not justified by competitor parity alone and requires stronger evidence of business value. |
| OS-03 | Advanced personalized habit recommendations | Potentially valuable, but effectiveness and implementation priorities require further validation. |
| OS-04 | Complex challenge modes with different goal-completion rules | Additional complexity that must be evaluated against MVP priorities, effort and evidence. |

These exclusions are specific to the proposed MVP. An excluded capability is not automatically approved for a future release.

## 3.3 Future Opportunities

Potential opportunities for later investigation include personalized habit recommendations, community functionality, shared challenges and advanced challenge modes.

For example, Easy and Hard challenge modes could be explored, with different goal-completion rules and associated milestones. These remain concepts requiring validation rather than committed delivery items.

Any future capability must be supported by evidence, business justification, feasibility assessment and formal prioritization.

## 3.4 Scope Change Control

Any request to add or modify scope must be assessed for:

- Contribution to business objectives.
- Evidence supporting the requirement.
- Development effort and technical dependencies.
- Budget and schedule impact.
- Testing, operational and user-experience risks.

The designated scope approver must authorize changes before they become committed requirements.

