# 5. Current State Analysis

## 5.1 Current User Journey

The current-state understanding is based on the available project scenario and initial investigation. The detailed user journey requires further validation.

1. Users complete onboarding and begin interacting with the application.
2. Users record or engage with health-related activities.
3. Engagement may decline during the first weeks after onboarding.
4. Some users disengage before developing a consistent habit-tracking routine.
5. The largest observed subscription conversion drop-off occurs before users initiate a paid subscription.

## 5.2 Observed Issues

### Declining Engagement

Users may find repetitive tracking difficult to sustain, forget to log activities or lose motivation after missed days.

### Difficulty Maintaining Routines

Users may struggle to resume consistent activity after interruptions. This supports investigating streak recovery and habit-continuity mechanisms, but does not independently establish their effectiveness.

### Subscription Conversion Drop-Off

The largest observed conversion drop-off occurs before users initiate a paid subscription. Perceived premium value, motivation and other barriers to conversion remain hypotheses requiring investigation.

### Unvalidated Feature Effectiveness

Habit tracking, streaks, reminders, milestones and rewards may support engagement, but the extent to which these features improve retention or paid conversion has not been established.

## 5.3 Business Impact

The current state presents a potential opportunity to improve the conversion of existing free users and reduce early disengagement.

However, the financial impact, revenue opportunity, customer acquisition cost (CAC) implications and customer lifetime value (CLTV) implications must be validated using appropriate data.

## 5.4 Evidence Gaps

The project must establish or validate:

- Current conversion, engagement and retention baselines.
- The main reasons users do not initiate paid subscriptions.
- The causes of declining engagement.
- Which user segments experience the greatest difficulties.
- Whether proposed features change user behavior and contribute to business outcomes.
- The financial viability of the proposed intervention.

# 6. Future State

## 6.1 Intended User Experience

Users should be able to record selected health habits, monitor their progress toward milestones and maintain engagement through an experience that supports routine-building.

The experience should make it straightforward to record activities, understand progress and resume routines after missed days. Streak pauses, grace periods, reminders and rewards should follow clearly defined rules that are validated before implementation.

## 6.2 Intended Business Outcomes

The business aims to improve free-to-paid subscription conversion by supporting meaningful engagement and retention.

The project will evaluate whether the proposed MVP contributes to improved conversion while remaining within approved scope, budget and delivery constraints.

## 6.3 Measurement and Decision-Making

The business should be able to evaluate outcomes using validated conversion, engagement and retention metrics compared against established baselines.

Experiment findings should inform whether the MVP should be iterated, expanded or discontinued. Positive results from a limited pilot should not automatically authorize a full rollout.

Expected effects on revenue, CAC, CLTV and profitability remain subject to financial validation.

